course/modules/CF/CF1/project.html:201:          <li><strong>Cash flows</strong> are indexed from <em>t</em> = 0. The flow at 0 is <code>−capex</code>; the flow at <em>t</em> ≥ 1 is the operating flow plus the working-capital flow. The flows are floats in cents and are not rounded.</li>
course/modules/CF/CF1/project.html:213:        <p>Each of these raises <code>ValueError</code>: a capex that is not a positive integer number of cents, so a float such as 1000.0, a boolean or a text is refused; a life that is not a whole number of at least 1; revenue or cash-cost lists with one entry too few or too many for the life, or with a negative or non-finite entry (zero is valid in both lists); a tax rate below 0 or at or above 1, or a working-capital share below 0 or at or above 1 (1.0 is refused for each, and 0 is valid); inflation at or below −1; a rate that is not finite (nan or infinity), or at or below −1, in an NPV, a discounted payback, a Fisher conversion or a Monte Carlo request; an NPV over an empty list of flows; an IRR request with fewer than two flows, or with every flow zero (an outlay followed only by zeros, such as −100 and 0, is not all zero, so it is valid and has no root); a payback with fewer than two flows, or whose first flow is not negative; a Monte Carlo request with fewer than two draws or a negative volatility; a project with any one of the seven fields missing. The autograder checks the exception type for each of them; the wording of a message is not graded. Just inside these bounds the input is valid: a discount rate of −0.999 in a payback or Monte Carlo request, an inflation of −0.999 in a Fisher conversion or in a project, and a first payback flow of −1e-9 cents (negative, so it is an outlay); each of these returns a value. An IRR request whose first flow is positive, such as 100 and −110, is also valid: its one root is 0.1.</p>
course/modules/CF/CF1/project.html:339:    print("IRR roots of the two-root flow, to 6 decimals:", [round(r, 6) for r in roots(TWO_ROOT_FLOWS)])
course/modules/CF/CF1/project.html:340:    print("IRR roots of the rung-1 project, to 6 decimals:", [round(r, 6) for r in roots(base)])
course/modules/CF/CF1/project.html:433:          <p>Each draw multiplies revenue by <em>m</em> = exp(0.2 <em>z</em> − 0.02), where <em>z</em> is one standard normal number from random.Random(42).gauss(0, 1); the expected value of <em>m</em> is one (<em>E[exp(0.2 z)] = exp(0.02)</em>). Volatility 0.2, seed 42, 2,000 draws. The mean NPV of the draws is 885.85 dollars, with a sample standard deviation of 2,445.52 dollars and a standard error of 54.68 dollars (Lab 3). The deterministic NPV is 926.00 dollars. The gap is 40.15 dollars (926.00 − 885.85, both printed to the cent), which is 0.73 standard errors (40.15 ÷ 54.68 = 0.73). Lab 3 prints two decimals, so these figures carry rounding of up to half a cent in each printed value. The share of negative draws is 787 of 2,000, or 39.35 percent. The lowest draw is −4,808.83 dollars and the highest 13,800.44 dollars.</p>
exit 0 (1 = no match)
